Patch summary:
The arm64 guest's 16K kernel no longer gets a second DRM card from vkms.
First-stage init loads every module of the 16K ramdisk, vkms among them, with
no options, so vkms created its default device (card1) before the vendor
init's "modprobe vkms.ko create_default_dev=0". Mesa reads each card's sysfs
uevent as it looks for the GPU, and SurfaceFlinger and apps were denied
reading vkms's at every boot. vkms.create_default_dev=0 on the kernel command
line reaches that first load (libmodprobe takes module options from
/proc/cmdline), so the 16K guest has the 4K and x86 guests' one card. vkms
stays in the 16K ramdisk: the vendor copy is the 4K build.

diff --git a/ika_arm64/BoardConfig.mk b/ika_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
--- a/ika_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
+++ b/ika_arm64/BoardConfig.mk
@@ -94,3 +94,12 @@
 TARGET_BOARD_INFO_FILES := \
     device/google/cuttlefish/shared/desktop/android-info.txt \
     device/google/cuttlefish/shared/desktop/android-info-asahi.txt
+
+# First-stage init loads every module of the 16K ramdisk with no options,
+# vkms among them, so vkms made its default device, a second DRM card, before
+# init.cutf_cvm.rc's "modprobe vkms.ko create_default_dev=0" (a no-op once it
+# is loaded). libmodprobe takes module options from the kernel command line,
+# first stage included. vkms stays in the 16K ramdisk, as the vendor copy is
+# the 4K build. The x86 and 4K kernels load vkms only through init.cutf_cvm.rc,
+# where the option changes nothing.
+BOARD_KERNEL_CMDLINE += vkms.create_default_dev=0
